var e=1,t=`quant-npv-01`,n=`quantitative-methods`,r=`NPV and IRR`,i=`The two standard ways to judge an investment from its cash flows, and why they can disagree.`,a=`core`,o=[`quant-tvm-01`],s=9,c=[`CFA-L1`,`CAIA-L1`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`npv-what`,body:`You have a set of cash flows: money out at the start, money in later. Two questions follow, and they are not the same question.

**"Does this create value?"** is answered by [[net-present-value]]. Discount every cash flow to today at your [[required-return]], add them up including the negative ones, and look at the sign. Positive means the investment is worth more than it costs. That is the whole test.

**"What return does this earn?"** is answered by [[internal-rate-of-return]]. It is the discount rate at which NPV comes out to exactly zero — the rate the investment itself implies, expressed as a percentage.

Both use the same inputs. They answer different questions, and when they disagree the disagreement is informative rather than a problem to be resolved.`},{type:`formula`,id:`npv-formula`,latex:`NPV = \\sum_{t=0}^{n} \\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}`,plainReading:`Net present value is the sum of every cash flow discounted back to today. The t=0 term is the initial outlay, which is divided by one and so enters at full face value — negative.`,variables:[{symbol:`CF_t`,meaning:`the cash flow at time t, negative for money paid out`},{symbol:`r`,meaning:`the [[required-return]] — a judgement, not a fact`}]},{type:`intuition`,id:`why-npv-decides`,body:`NPV is measured in money, and IRR in percent, and that difference is the whole reason NPV is the better decision rule.

**A percentage cannot tell you how much value was created.** A 60% return on $1,000 makes you $600. A 12% return on $50 million makes you $6 million. IRR ranks the first higher; NPV correctly identifies the second as vastly more valuable. If you can only do one, and both are available, the percentage is the wrong tiebreaker.

There is a subtler problem too. IRR implicitly assumes every interim cash flow is reinvested at the IRR itself. If a fund returns capital early at a 40% IRR, that number quietly assumes you had somewhere else earning 40% to put it. Usually you did not, so the IRR overstates what you actually compounded.

None of this makes IRR useless. It is the language the private markets are conducted in and you cannot avoid it. But when the two conflict, NPV is the one that answers the question you care about.`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-npv`,body:`An investment costs $1,000 today and pays $400 at the end of each of the next three years. Your required return is 10%.`,walkthrough:[`Discount each inflow: 400/1.10 = 363.64, 400/1.21 = 330.58, 400/1.331 = 300.53.`,`Sum the inflows: 363.64 + 330.58 + 300.53 = **$994.74**.`,`Subtract the outlay: 994.74 - 1,000 = **NPV of -$5.26**.`,`**So reject it.** Marginally — but the sign is the answer, and it is negative. The $1,200 of total inflows against a $1,000 outlay looks like a clear profit until you account for when the money arrives.`,`Now find the [[internal-rate-of-return]]: the rate that would make this exactly break even. It is about **9.7%**, just below the 10% required return — which is the same conclusion the NPV reached, stated as a percentage instead of a dollar amount.`,`Notice the two agree here, and notice *why*: it is a single project with one sign change in its cash flows and no scale comparison involved. That is the case where IRR is well behaved.`]},{type:`concept`,id:`when-they-disagree`,body:`IRR misleads in four recognisable situations, and it is worth being able to name them.

**Scale.** IRR is blind to size. A small project with a high percentage return can rank above a large one that creates far more value.

**Timing.** IRR rewards getting money back quickly. A fund that returns capital in year two and stops can post a higher IRR than one that compounds patiently for a decade and returns far more in total.

**Multiple sign changes.** If cash flows go negative, positive, then negative again — a fund with a follow-on capital call, say — there can be more than one rate at which NPV equals zero. Two mathematically valid IRRs, neither of them meaningful.

**The reinvestment assumption.** As above: IRR assumes interim distributions were reinvested at the IRR. The more front-loaded the cash flows, the more that assumption is doing the work.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`sign-convention`,body:`**The initial outlay must be negative.** It is the most common NPV error and it is silent — the arithmetic produces a plausible number, just the wrong one.

A good habit: before computing anything, write the cash flows out as a row with explicit signs and check the pattern looks like the investment you think you are valuing. Money out first, money in later, one sign change. If you see a different pattern than you expected, find out why before you compute a rate.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`In private markets IRR is the currency of the conversation, so knowing its failure modes matters more here than almost anywhere.

**IRR flatters early distributions.** A manager who exits one investment quickly and profitably can carry a strong fund IRR for years on the strength of it, even if the rest of the portfolio is unremarkable. This is why IRR is always read next to a multiple of invested capital — the multiple is blind to timing, IRR is obsessed with it, and you need both.

**Subscription lines change the number.** Borrowing at the fund level to delay calling investor capital shortens the period over which IRR is measured, which raises it, without changing a single underlying investment. Whether a reported IRR is levered by a credit facility is a fair and important question.

**The [[j-curve|J-curve]] is IRR being honest.** Early-life IRRs are negative because fees are charged before value is realised. A young fund's IRR is close to meaningless, and reading it as a verdict on the manager is a beginner's error.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`NPV answers "does this create value?" in money. IRR answers "what rate does this imply?" in percent.`,`When they conflict, follow NPV. A percentage cannot express how much value was created.`,`IRR is blind to scale, rewards early cash flows, can have multiple solutions, and assumes reinvestment at itself.`,`In private markets, always read IRR alongside a multiple — one is obsessed with timing, the other ignores it.`]}],u=[{id:`quant-npv-01-q1`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:2,tags:[`npv`,`discounting`],concept:`npv-formula`,stem:`An investment costs $1,000 today and returns $400 at the end of each of the next 3 years. At a 10% required return, what is its NPV?`,answer:-5.26,tolerance:1,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`$`,inputHint:`Answer in dollars, to the nearest cent. Use a minus sign if negative.`,explanation:`Inflows discount to 363.64 + 330.58 + 300.53 = $994.74, against a $1,000 outlay, so NPV = -$5.26. The $1,200 of raw inflows looks profitable until the timing is priced in.`},{id:`quant-npv-01-q2`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:1,tags:[`npv`,`decision-rule`],concept:`npv-what`,stem:`What does a positive NPV mean?`,choices:[`The investment returns more than the required return, so it adds value`,`The investment's total cash inflows exceed its outflows`,`The investment's IRR is above zero`,`The investment will definitely be profitable`],answerIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. NPV is positive exactly when the cash flows are worth more than the outlay once discounted at the required return — which is the definition of adding value.`,`Raw inflows exceeding outflows is neither necessary nor sufficient. The worked example had $1,200 in against $1,000 out and a negative NPV.`,`An IRR above zero only means you got more nominal money back than you put in. It says nothing about clearing your required return.`,`NPV is computed from *projected* cash flows. It says the investment is attractive given those projections, not that the projections are right.`],explanation:`NPV compares value created against the return you demanded for the risk. Both halves matter: the cash flows and the rate you chose to judge them by.`},{id:`quant-npv-01-q3`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`irr`],concept:`npv-what`,stem:`An investment costs $100 today and pays $60 at the end of year 1 and $60 at the end of year 2. What is its IRR, in percent?`,answer:13.07,tolerance:.3,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`%`,inputHint:`Answer in percent, to 1 or 2 decimal places.`,explanation:`IRR solves -100 + 60/(1+r) + 60/(1+r)^2 = 0, giving r = 13.07%. There is no clean algebraic route for most cash flow patterns — in practice you let a solver find it, but you should still be able to sanity check the magnitude: $120 back on $100 over two years has to be somewhere in the low teens.`},{id:`quant-npv-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`irr`,`scale`,`decision-rule`],concept:`why-npv-decides`,stem:`Project A: invest $1,000, IRR 60%, NPV $400. Project B: invest $50m, IRR 12%, NPV $6m. You can do both. Which should you do?`,choices:[`Project A only — a 60% IRR is far superior`,`Both, since both have positive NPV`,`Project B only — the larger NPV dominates`,`Neither can be judged without knowing the discount rate used`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`This is the scale trap. A 60% return on $1,000 creates $400 of value; ranking it above $6m of value creation mistakes the unit for the magnitude.`,`Correct, and the question is a trap in both directions. The projects are not mutually exclusive, so the answer is not a ranking at all — every positive-NPV project you can fund should be funded.`,`B creates far more value, but there is no reason to skip A: it also creates value, and doing it costs you nothing you needed for B.`,`The discount rate is already embedded in the NPV figures given. Refusing to decide is not the same as being rigorous.`],explanation:`Two lessons at once. IRR is blind to scale, so it cannot rank projects of different sizes — but also, ranking is only necessary when choices are mutually exclusive. When they are not, the rule is simply: take every positive NPV.`},{id:`quant-npv-01-q5`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`irr`,`reinvestment`,`misconception`],concept:`why-npv-decides`,stem:`"A fund reporting a 30% IRR has compounded its investors' money at 30% a year." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — IRR assumes interim distributions were reinvested at 30%, which the investor generally could not do`,`False — IRR is always overstated because it ignores fees`,`True — that is precisely the definition of IRR`,`False — because IRR cannot be computed for a fund with irregular cash flows`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. IRR is the rate that makes the fund's own cash flows break even; it embeds an assumption that money returned early kept earning at that same rate. If it instead sat in cash, the investor's actual compounded return was lower.`,`Fees matter, but IRRs are usually quoted net of them, and it is not the reason the statement fails. The reinvestment assumption is.`,`This is the misconception. IRR is a property of the cash flow stream, not a description of what the investor's capital actually did.`,`IRR handles irregular cash flows perfectly well — that is precisely why private markets use it rather than a time-weighted measure.`],explanation:`The gap between a reported IRR and what an investor genuinely compounded is largest when distributions came early and the investor had nowhere comparable to redeploy them. This is not a technicality; it is the main reason IRR flatters funds that exit quickly.`},{id:`quant-npv-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`irr`,`multiple-irr`],concept:`when-they-disagree`,stem:`A fund's cash flows are: -$100 (year 0), +$230 (year 1), -$132 (year 2). What is notable about its IRR?`,choices:[`There is no IRR, because the final cash flow is negative`,`There are two mathematically valid IRRs, so no single one is meaningful`,`The IRR equals the average of the cash flows divided by the outlay`,`The IRR is negative, since the cash flows sum to a loss`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`An IRR exists — in fact more than one does. A negative final cash flow does not prevent a solution, it just breaks uniqueness.`,`Correct. Two sign changes admit up to two roots, and this pattern has them (10% and 20%). Neither is a sensible answer to "what return did this earn?", which is the point.`,`IRR is not an average of anything. It is the root of a polynomial in the discount rate.`,`The cash flows do sum to -$2, but that is not how IRR is determined, and it is not negative here — there are two positive roots.`],explanation:`Each sign change in a cash flow series admits another possible root. Solving -100x^2 + 230x - 132 = 0 for x = 1+r gives x = 1.10 and x = 1.20 exactly, so NPV is zero at both 10% and 20%. Funds with follow-on capital calls after distributions can genuinely produce this pattern, and it is the clearest case where IRR should simply be set aside in favour of NPV.`},{id:`quant-npv-01-q7`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`irr`,`on-the-job`,`alternatives`],concept:`on-the-job`,stem:`A private equity fund uses a subscription credit line to delay calling investor capital by nine months. Nothing about the underlying investments changes. What happens to the reported net IRR?`,choices:[`It falls, because the fund pays interest on the credit line`,`It rises, because IRR is measured over a shorter period of investor capital at risk`,`It is unchanged, because the underlying investments are identical`,`It cannot be computed until the line is repaid`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`The interest cost is real and does drag slightly on absolute returns, but it is small relative to the effect of compressing the measurement period.`,`Correct. IRR is acutely sensitive to timing. Shortening the time investor money is outstanding raises the annualised rate even though the underlying assets performed identically.`,`The investments are identical; the *cash flow timing between fund and investor* is not, and IRR is computed from exactly those flows.`,`The IRR is computable throughout. The question is whether it is comparable to a fund that does not use a line — and it is not.`],explanation:`This is why "is that IRR levered by a subscription line?" is a standard diligence question, and why multiples are quoted alongside. A multiple of invested capital is immune to this effect, which is exactly what makes the pair informative.`},{id:`quant-npv-01-q8`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`npv`,`sensitivity`],concept:`npv-formula`,stem:`An analyst computes an NPV of +$2m using a 9% discount rate. A colleague argues the correct rate is 12%. What can you say without recomputing?`,choices:[`The NPV at 12% will be lower, and may well be negative`,`The NPV at 12% will be higher, since a higher rate means higher returns`,`The NPV is unchanged, because the cash flows have not changed`,`The IRR must be below 9%, since the NPV was positive`],answerIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. NPV falls as the discount rate rises, and a +$2m result at 9% gives no guarantee of surviving 12% — particularly if the cash flows are long-dated.`,`This confuses the rate you *demand* with the return you *earn*. Demanding more makes a fixed set of future cash flows less attractive today.`,`The cash flows are fixed but the NPV is a function of both cash flows and rate. Change either and the answer changes.`,`Backwards. A positive NPV at 9% means the IRR is *above* 9% — the investment clears that hurdle.`],explanation:`NPV is monotonically decreasing in the discount rate, which is why the choice of rate deserves as much scrutiny as the cash flow forecast. Note the useful corollary: a positive NPV at some rate means the IRR exceeds that rate.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
